  "full_response": "The SOLID principles are a set of five design principles for writing maintainable and scalable object-oriented software. They are:\n\n1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):\n   SRP states that a class should have only one responsibility, meaning it should take care of one task or functionality within a software system. This principle aims to increase cohesion and decrease coupling within a software design.\n\n2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP):\n   OCP states that reusable software components should be open for extension but closed for modification. This means that when adding new functionality or features, the existing components' codebase should not need to be changed, breaking the existing functionality or introducing dependency issues.\n\n3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):\n   LSP states that objects of a base class (subclass) should be replaceable with objects of its subclasses without affecting the correctness of a program. In other words, subclasses should be able to substitute the base class without causing any issues.\n\n4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):\n   ISP states that a class should not depend upon the implementation details of another class. Instead, it should depend upon only the methods that are required by the class itself, and not the other methods. To simplify, software interfaces should be small, focused, and feature-specific.\n\n5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):\n   DIP states that high-level modules/belongings (such as business logic) should not depend on low-level modules (like data access), and both should depend on abstractions. This principle emphasizes minimizing the dependencies between components, making them easier to change, and allows for more flexibility in designing systems. This can be achieved through the use of dependency injection, which helps in reusing the same object throughout an application's lifecycle as well as reducing tight coupling between components.",
